<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Account, session and character storage, shared by the login server and (read
    only) the game server.

    A NEW library rather than a folder in ...Multiplayer: that project's csproj
    states it deliberately has no references, and one of its files is source-linked
    into the net35 BepInEx mod. Adding a NuGet package there would break that
    contract and drag a database driver towards the mod build.

    PostgreSQL over loopback rather than an embedded file database. The servers run
    under Wine, and Npgsql is 100% managed C#: no native binary, no runtimes/ folder
    to lose in a deploy, and therefore no DllNotFoundException on a server that
    started up looking healthy. Two processes sharing one database is also just what
    a server database does, rather than something we would have to keep re-proving.
    Postgres already runs on the target box, so this is a database and a role, not
    new infrastructure.

    PIN THE VERSION. Do not run `dotnet add package Npgsql` and take what it offers:
    the current default resolves to a line with no net6.0 target. 8.0.7 has a real
    lib/net6.0 and, verified against the package contents, ships no native files at
    all.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net6.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <Platforms>AnyCPU;x64;x86</Platforms>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Npgsql" Version="8.0.7" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
